an II (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanotan_II"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Tan & skin tone","chain":["Melanotan injected","Melanocytes make more melanin","Darker skin without sun"],"effect":"indirect","text":"Melanotan, a synthetic alpha-MSH analogue, is injected to prompt melanocytes to make more melanin and darken skin with less or no sun. Its pigment-raising action is established; the tanning use is unlicensed and health agencies have warned against it since 2007."},"wikipedia":{"title":"Sunless tanning","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunless_tanning"},"grokipedia":null,"lead":["\"Tan jabs\" are unlicensed injectable products, usually called melanotan, sold online and in some gyms and salons to darken skin without sun exposure. They are synthetic analogues of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH), which drives melanin production.[1][2]","They have never been approved as tanning drugs. Health agencies began warning against them in 2007, and the US FDA issued a warning letter in 2009.[2] A 2009 BMJ report linked an unlicensed \"sun tan jab\" to changes in moles.[3] Related approved drugs exist for other uses: afamelanotide for a rare light-sensitivity disorder, and bremelanotide for hypoactive sexual desire disorder in premenopausal women.[1]"],"infobox":[["Type","Unlicensed injectable peptide"],["Class","Alpha-MSH analogue (melanotan)"],["Approval for tanning","None"],["Warnings","Agencies from 2007"]],"sections":[{"h":"Origins","paras":["Alpha-MSH's role in pigmentation was known from the 1960s, and University of Arizona researchers developed analogues in the 1980s, including melanotan I (afamelanotide).[1] Melanotan II was later licensed to Palatin for sexual dysfunction, with development dropped around 2000 in favour of bremelanotide.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Reported effects and risks","paras":["Reported effects include facial flushing, nausea, vomiting, yawning and appetite loss, and erections in men.[2] The 2009 BMJ report described changes to moles linked to an unlicensed tan jab.[3] Moles and freckles may darken.[2] Evidence on melanoma is unclear: a 2013 review found no conclusive proof of a causal link, and a 2021 review suggested higher melanoma rates among users could probably be explained by greater UV exposure.[2] Warnings were issued by agencies including the Danish Medicines Agency, UK MHRA, US FDA, Irish Medicines Board and Norwegian Medicines Agency.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found for unlicensed tanning use. The 2009 BMJ item is a case report of mole changes.[3]"],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"1980s","event":"University of Arizona researchers develop alpha-MSH analogues.[1]"},{"year":"2007","event":"Health agencies begin warning against unlicensed products.[2]"},{"year":"2009","event":"BMJ report on mole changes; FDA warning letter.[2][3]"},{"year":"2019","event":"FDA approves bremelanotide for a sexual desire disorder.[1]"}],"refs":[{"title":"Sunless tanning","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunless_tanning","source":"Wikipedia","year":2025,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Melanotan II","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanotan_II","source":"Wikipedia","year":2025,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Change in moles linked to use of unlicensed \"sun tan jab\"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19174439/","source":"BMJ","year":2009,"type":"study"}],"studies":[{"title":"Change in moles linked to use of unlicensed \"sun tan jab\"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19174439/","source":"BMJ","year":2009,"kind":null}],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Melanotan 2 Risk VS Reward?","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/melanotan-2-risk-vs-reward.1771201/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20251228034959/https://looksmax.org/threads/melanotan-2-risk-vs-reward.1771201/"},{"title":"Melanotan 1 with freckles","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/melanotan-1-with-freckles.1750681/","site":"looksmax.org"